Route the TLS root store to OpenSSL's default paths on iOS.

Wesnoth's Apple branch calls SecTrustCopyAnchorCertificates() to enumerate the
system trust store. That API is macOS-only -- it does not exist on iOS, where the
build fails outright with "use of undeclared identifier". The Security framework
header is fine to include; only this entry point is absent.

The existing #else branch already does the portable thing, ctx.set_default_
verify_paths(), so iOS takes that rather than growing a third implementation.
Note the consequence: certificate verification then uses OpenSSL's configured CA
bundle instead of the iOS system trust store, which is the same posture every
other OpenSSL consumer in Xios already has.

--- a/src/tls_root_store.cpp
+++ b/src/tls_root_store.cpp
@@ -18,7 +18,7 @@
 
 #ifdef _WIN32
 #include <wincrypt.h>
-#elif defined(__APPLE__)
+#elif defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(__IPHONEOS__)
 #include <Security/Security.h>
 #endif
 
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@
 	CertCloseStore(hStore, 0);
 
 	SSL_CTX_set_cert_store(ctx.native_handle(), store);
-#elif defined(__APPLE__)
+#elif defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(__IPHONEOS__)
 	X509_STORE *store = X509_STORE_new();
 	CFArrayRef certs = NULL;
 	// copy all system certs
